WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:10.920
Malcolm Grant: We move, then, to the next
item on the agenda, which is the strategic

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:16.720
framework for specialised services, and I
am very pleased to welcome to the table Jonathan

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.510
Fielden, who is our Director of Specialised
Commissioning. Jonathan, I would like to invite

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:26.609
you to introduce the paper, and then perhaps
invite Noel Gordon, who is the Chair of the

00:00:26.609 --> 00:00:32.769
Specialised Commissioning Committee, to follow.
Jonathan Fielden: Thank you very much. Specialised

00:00:32.769 --> 00:00:38.360
commissioning, which covers the 146 prescribed
services the Secretary of State tells us to

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:44.470
commission, covers from the rarest of conditions
through to many conditions which affect more

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:49.600
patients across the country, but are very
often high cost, that need to be located with

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.190
highly specialised teams to get the care the
patients require and the outcomes – and

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:56.400
quality outcomes – that we need.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.680
So in the two months that I have been in post
full‑time, being out and about round the

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.790
regions with colleagues and patients, one
of the coherent questions has been, ‘Well,

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:11.890
how does specialised fit in with the Five
Year Forward View?’ And that is what we

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:17.380
are aiming to address now and over the next
three months as we take this framework out.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:23.560
Very clearly, we are linked to the three parts
of the vision of the Five Year Forward View:

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:25.689
better health, better care and better value.

00:01:25.689 --> 00:01:32.990
On better health, the health and wellbeing
gap: for specialised, getting into prevention,

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:37.350
most of the patients are quite a long way
down the pathway. For us, this will be about

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:42.030
focusing on the personalisation and precision
agenda, wherein first we will be reducing

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:48.530
harm to patients so that we can focus the
treatment for these conditions in a better

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:54.530
way for them. That will also allow us to treat
more patients by focusing the care in the

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.020
best way.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:02:02.299
The second aspect around the care and quality
gap is there has been a lot of advantage in

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:08.690
bringing the specialised commissioning nationally
to get the national consistency of process

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:14.810
and outcomes, the national clinical advice.
However, that has fragmented some of the pathways.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:19.409
When you fragment pathways, from patients’
perspective that does not deliver the experience,

00:02:19.409 --> 00:02:24.390
or necessarily the outcomes, that they are
after. So we need to link those pathways back

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:25.310
together.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:31.830
Then, very importantly, about the finance
and efficiency gap. Specialised commissioning

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:38.099
is £15.66 billion; it is 14% of the NHS budget.
It is vitally important that that money is

00:02:38.099 --> 00:02:43.709
utilised in the most effective way to give
best value for patients. As we go ahead, the

00:02:43.709 --> 00:02:47.890
challenges in that budget are as great or
greater than many other aspects. However,

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:52.769
by controlling that in the way of getting
best outcomes for patients, we can ensure

00:02:52.769 --> 00:02:57.970
that other patients outside the specialised
arena also get the care that they require.

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:03.450
So the framework has three broad areas: how
do we link into the place- and population-based

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:08.329
care; how do we provide the national support
to ensure the quality of outcomes; and how

00:03:08.329 --> 00:03:13.970
do we maintain the financial grip and value
across that? So, in the first area, there

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:17.549
are three aspects. We are working closely
with our STP colleagues – we have just heard

00:03:17.549 --> 00:03:26.670
about the STP process – on the patient,
personal focus: what are the populations of

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:32.269
specialised which are best for us to be working
closely with you on?

00:03:32.269 --> 00:03:39.349
With that, then what is the provider footprint,
the provider configuration, that is needed

00:03:39.349 --> 00:03:43.760
to deliver that in the best way for your area?
That will lead to some changes.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:51.900
Then thirdly, the collaborative commissioning:
how do we work best with our CCG, STP and

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:57.920
hub colleagues around the models of commissioning
to get the best outcomes for our patients?

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:03.519
With national‑level support, we need to
have consistent national-level clinical leadership

00:04:03.519 --> 00:04:11.930
and advice. We are just completing the reconfiguration
of our clinical reference groups to ensure

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:17.560
we have the right clinical and patient/public
voice support at national level to get those

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:19.940
consistent outcomes.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:24.160
There is a lot to do on our business information;
making sure we have got the right information

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:29.440
to deliver the change that we need for our
patients. We need to ensure that we are working

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:35.190
closely with research. Specialised commissioning
houses many of the most innovative aspects

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:42.320
of health across the NHS, and how we best
focus our resources and link to that agenda,

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:47.800
and bring treatments to the earliest point
that work, but also mindful that we will be

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:52.040
stopping some treatments that no longer work
effectively, or processes that no longer work

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:57.050
effectively, and linking that back to our
clinical and patients’ advice on that.

00:04:57.050 --> 00:05:04.510
Then finally, around financing. For finances,
it is vitally important we maintain that national

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:10.270
grip that we have had in landing the budget
for 2015 in 2016, which is a great improvement

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:14.940
over the last two to three years, but focusing
those resources in the best way to get the

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.820
best outcomes for our patients.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:21.950
So what will look different from the patient
perspective? We want to work with our patient

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:27.060
public – our citizens – to ensure that
we are defining, over the next two or three

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:32.290
months, the outcomes they wish to see from
specialised. We want to make sure those pathways

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:36.030
are linked, and linked back together. For
our clinicians, we want to make sure that

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:42.270
they have that consistent national approach,
but that they have very clearly‑defined

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:50.560
pathways located back clearly to where patients
need that care. For our provider and commissioning

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:55.510
colleagues, it is making sure we have the
right configuration of providers and the right

00:05:55.510 --> 00:06:00.740
mechanisms of commissioning, working particularly
with our STP – our sustainability and transformation

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:05.840
– colleagues to ensure that this the best
way, within the resource that we have, of

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:09.430
delivering the outcomes of care that our patients
wish to see.

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:14.710
Malcolm Grant: Good. Jonathan, thank you very
much for the introduction. Let me open it

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:20.870
now for questions and discussion from around
the table. Bruce, and then Michelle.

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:27.650
Bruce Keogh: Jonathan, in terms of linking
this to the Five Year Forward View, I think

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:33.870
there are some other aspects which are worthy
of consideration. The first is the role of

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:39.470
specialised commissioning in rolling out innovation,
and that may sometimes be within the context

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:45.810
of a rapid response, or a response to some
kind of emergency.

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:52.650
I would just like to bring to the Board’s
attention: 2009 there was a terrible flu epidemic,

00:06:52.650 --> 00:07:00.020
and we needed to have more beds in the country
to provide a complicated service called extracorporeal

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:08.620
membrane oxygenation. There were only two
beds in the country. By using the processes

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:14.630
which are embedded in specialised commissioning;
in other words, by defining specifications

00:07:14.630 --> 00:07:20.280
and by introducing very focused clinical leadership,
in this case under the leadership of a single

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:25.590
individual called Richard Firmin from Leicester,
we were able to roll out from two beds to

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:34.690
about 60 beds in a matter of a few weeks.
The end result of that was we had some of

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:39.720
the best results for extracorporeal membrane
oxygenation in the world, where our national

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:45.640
results were equivalent to the results of
the best single centres in the United States.

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:51.150
I think we learned a lot from that about how
we can use the offices and the processes of

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:55.840
specialised commissioning for pushing out
innovation when it needs to be, very, very

00:07:55.840 --> 00:08:00.680
quickly across the NHS.
Jonathan Fielden: I think you are very right

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:05.550
on that, Bruce. The national consistency in
the national standards, the ability to roll

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:10.220
out at pace, but then linking in very much
to how it is delivered locally so patients

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:13.070
get the care at the quality and speed they
need.

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:17.490
Malcolm Grant: Michelle.
Michelle Mitchell: Thank you. You have described

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:22.460
the strategic framework very well. Could you
just talk us through how you will be developing

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:27.900
the outcomes of the strategy over the next
couple of months, and in particular how you

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:36.959
plan to engage, involve, co-produce the strategy
with citizens and how they will be involved

00:08:36.959 --> 00:08:42.919
in the implementation of the policy?
Jonathan Fielden: So already we have floated

00:08:42.919 --> 00:08:47.629
what you see before you with our patient/public
voice group. They were some of the first people

00:08:47.629 --> 00:08:53.829
we involved with this, and with our clinical
colleagues before that. The sense is that,

00:08:53.829 --> 00:08:57.540
yes, this is in the right direction, but we
need to put some more detail around it; hence

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:03.360
over the next two to three months working
with our patient/public voice, our strategic

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:10.310
partners, stakeholder groupings and with our
clinical groupings to put the detail around,

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:14.589
so that we can get outcomes that matter to
patients, as well as the outcomes which matter

00:09:14.589 --> 00:09:19.250
to our clinicians, right.
The clinical reference groups, in particular,

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:26.360
will be key. We have focused those around
the priorities that we need for the NHS, and

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:32.019
we are appointing the chairs and the patient
and public voice colleagues into that, and

00:09:32.019 --> 00:09:35.529
we have firmed that up.
Around our prioritisation, we are just about

00:09:35.529 --> 00:09:44.730
the land the prioritisation process for CPAG,
and that again enhances the amount of public

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:54.420
involvement within what happens on specialised.
We are very keen that we see this as a dynamic

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:58.889
process of confirming how we link to the Five
Year Forward View but see patients absolutely

00:09:58.889 --> 00:10:03.620
at the heart, defining the outcomes experience
that they need; particularly in the areas

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:11.389
of rare disease, where really it is a process
of how those rare diseases matter to patients

00:10:11.389 --> 00:10:14.720
that we need to be linking to them to get
the best value for them.

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:19.040
Michelle Mitchell: Okay. Thank you.
Malcolm Grant: Moira.

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:25.829
Moira Gibb: Thanks. Thanks, Jonathan. Obviously
sounds, sitting here, very sensible, and the

00:10:25.829 --> 00:10:31.529
national excellence and local connection.
However, it might look a bit different for

00:10:31.529 --> 00:10:36.490
CCGs; how are they seeing this?
Jonathan Fielden: So in the last six months

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:44.180
we have revamped our collaborative commissioning,
and we are working closely with CCGs and Dr

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:51.810
Nick Harding from the Midlands, in particular,
and the NHS clinical commissioners to see

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:55.939
how we can best do this, and we will continue
to work with them.

00:10:55.939 --> 00:11:04.779
I think there are three aspects to that: the
desire that our CCG colleagues and STP colleagues

00:11:04.779 --> 00:11:09.829
have in this area, their capability and their
capacity to do so. That varies. We have some

00:11:09.829 --> 00:11:14.430
areas of the country, such as Manchester and,
increasingly, Southeast London, where they

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:20.680
are advanced in both those areas. Others are
moving along in all those three aspects. Very

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:28.249
much we want in every STP for our colleagues
to be at the table for these discussions.

00:11:28.249 --> 00:11:33.360
We want them to be involved in discussions
as we move, with greater capability and greater

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:38.100
capacity, such as with Manchester, towards
more delegated and devolved. There are legal

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:42.089
aspects, and we are working very closely,
particularly with Manchester, to make sure

00:11:42.089 --> 00:11:46.980
that we get this right; to have the national
consistency, but delivered in a way which

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:58.790
links in locally.
Noel Gordon: I should just address somewhat

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:03.399
more contextually what we have been doing
in the Specialised Commissioning Committee,

00:12:03.399 --> 00:12:08.449
Malcolm. As you know, since it was set up
eight months ago we have probably devoted

00:12:08.449 --> 00:12:14.660
the majority of our time to thinking through
what is the strategic framework that will

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:22.100
keep us in line with the Five Year Forward
View and help us implement many of the complexities

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:31.139
that we face. What this paper sets out I would
characterise as our base camp two. We are

00:12:31.139 --> 00:12:40.019
on the way toward a more sophisticated model
of commissioning. We are addressing the triple‑witching

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:47.740
of very fast-rising demand, a huge pipeline
coming forward of innovative drugs and technologies

00:12:47.749 --> 00:12:53.990
– each of those drugs gets more expensive
– and a supply configuration that is not

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:59.230
ideal. It is fragmented in places and which
we want to re-join for a number of different

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:06.769
pathways. Unravelling that triple‑witching
is the start that we have made today with

00:13:06.769 --> 00:13:13.629
this strategic framework.
There is a way to go. We have still a backlog

00:13:13.629 --> 00:13:20.260
of policies that we need to deliver on. We
still have to do work on the co-commissioning

00:13:20.269 --> 00:13:26.100
structure and exactly how we will commission
across different levels and possibilities

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:34.110
that we have within it. Also, how we deal
with our own prioritisation processes for

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:38.949
new drugs and devices that become available
each year, through our commissioning intentions

00:13:38.949 --> 00:13:45.620
policies, for which we have indeed engaged
the public voice, at least on one occasion

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:50.499
recently and now a second occasion that is
going through a consultation process, which

00:13:50.499 --> 00:14:00.889
will be more or less completed by next week.
So what we have got is, I think, a hugely

00:14:00.889 --> 00:14:08.100
progressive start on rebuilding the way we
approach specialised commissioning in the

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:16.459
NHS. The destination is one that we need to
fix upon totally collaboratively, and with

00:14:16.459 --> 00:14:21.790
many active patients’ groups who are guiding
us. However, I think today is a really good

00:14:21.790 --> 00:14:25.290
start. There is a lot of implementation to
do, there is a lot of heavy lifting to do

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:30.709
in the implementation, but it is giving us,
perhaps for the first time in relatively recent

00:14:30.709 --> 00:14:35.459
memory, a very clear journey and direction.
Malcolm Grant: John.

00:14:35.459 --> 00:14:41.559
John Burn: Thank you, great summary. Obviously
I sit as a non-exec on your committee, so

00:14:41.559 --> 00:14:47.759
I have the inside track on some of the conversations.
However, as a clinical geneticist I have always

00:14:47.759 --> 00:14:52.290
had to give voice to the people with rare
diseases, most of which are genetic, as you

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:56.980
know. It is worth perhaps just teasing out:
we talk about 7,000 rare diseases, but some

00:14:56.980 --> 00:15:01.040
of them are not that rare; the hereditary
cancers, cystic fibrosis, sickle-cell disease

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:05.869
and so on. However, there are some that are
vanishingly rare, and giving voice to that

00:15:05.869 --> 00:15:11.749
community can be challenging when they often
feel that their treatments are not being pushed

00:15:11.749 --> 00:15:16.360
to the front because of that budgetary grip
that you have to maintain.

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:24.889
So I think there are two aspects to the question:
first of all, how do we protect national provision

00:15:24.889 --> 00:15:28.910
for some of these very rare conditions; like,
say, the mitochondrial diseases or the muscular

00:15:28.910 --> 00:15:35.519
dystrophies, where it is just not practical
for STPs, CCGs or any other segmental group

00:15:35.519 --> 00:15:40.160
to look after them? So we need to think about
it at national level. How do we protect them?

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:43.990
Because obviously they can help with that
budgetary management to some extent.

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:50.749
However, also we are all aware of the dramatic
expansion of this field. We are finding new

00:15:50.749 --> 00:15:57.629
rare diseases on a weekly basis now, thanks
to the advances in genomics. So this 14% figure:

00:15:57.629 --> 00:16:03.639
I have asked in the past, where did we get
14% from? It is almost inevitable that that

00:16:03.639 --> 00:16:08.369
is going to come under specific pressure as
these very rare conditions start to become

00:16:08.369 --> 00:16:13.220
treatable with, often, very expensive drugs.
So how do we look after the ‘very rare’

00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:18.629
rare diseases, and how do we handle this question
of the fact we might need a bit more money?

00:16:18.629 --> 00:16:24.139
Jonathan Fielden: Thanks, John. Absolutely.
For all of those, it is vitally important

00:16:24.139 --> 00:16:29.959
that we keep that national approach, that
national consistency, because it is only by

00:16:29.959 --> 00:16:35.269
that that we will get the outcomes and the
experience that our patients require, so they

00:16:35.269 --> 00:16:39.550
will need that national approach. Some of
the conditions will link more closely to a

00:16:39.550 --> 00:16:44.420
larger footprint such as a Manchester size,
for example. So there will be a national approach,

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:48.639
there will be a regional hub approach, and
then there will be those conditions which

00:16:48.639 --> 00:16:53.040
are more suited to the STP, so we need to
make sure we get that right, that we do not

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:57.649
lose quality and consistency of that.
I think where you identify new challenges

00:16:57.649 --> 00:17:03.029
for us, through many of the innovations and
research side, much of which also sits within

00:17:03.029 --> 00:17:10.750
those doing specialised care, it is vitally
important that we use the personalisation

00:17:10.750 --> 00:17:16.270
to focus on the treatments that work. We monitor,
we research and we analyse to make sure that

00:17:16.270 --> 00:17:22.270
they continue to work; and in that way, that
allows us to free up budget from those areas

00:17:22.270 --> 00:17:26.560
where perhaps things do not work. We need
to work with our clinical colleagues and the

00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:31.840
public very clearly about not doing the things
that do not work, to allow us the space and

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:37.950
capacity to do the things that do work; innovative
ways of working with industry and pharma around

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:43.670
how we pay for this over a longer period of
time, with new ways of looking at that, because

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:48.910
only that way will we be able to get the treatments
we need to the patients that need it, but

00:17:48.910 --> 00:17:54.460
in a way that does not mean that the specialised
budget sucks everything away from the rest

00:17:54.460 --> 00:17:59.070
of health. It is vitally important that we
hold within the specialised budget – 7%

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:05.570
increase this year, 4.8% next year, 4.5% for
the next two, and then 5% – so the rest

00:18:05.570 --> 00:18:10.880
of the NHS can get the funds they need for
the transformative change that is required

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:14.840
there too.
David Roberts: I guess, as you would expect

00:18:14.840 --> 00:18:20.330
as the Chair of the Audit Committee, that
that is a material area of focus. Because

00:18:20.330 --> 00:18:29.250
on the one hand, this is hugely exciting:
we can bring better treatment to patients.

00:18:29.250 --> 00:18:33.940
On the other hand, it scares the living daylights
of me a bit, about the pipeline of what is

00:18:33.940 --> 00:18:40.510
coming down the track and the potential costs
and crowding‑out of that on other things.

00:18:40.510 --> 00:18:50.670
I guess really to you and to Paul, is: how
can you assure the Board and me that we are

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:57.370
going to be able to keep within the financial
envelope you describe, which is already twice

00:18:57.370 --> 00:19:03.190
the growth that the rest of the Service broadly
gets? So specialised is continuing to get

00:19:03.190 --> 00:19:10.980
a bigger share of the pie, and that is appropriate,
but clearly the risk in this is very high,

00:19:10.980 --> 00:19:16.420
so the assurance I guess we are looking for
from you two is about how we keep financial

00:19:16.420 --> 00:19:18.770
distance.
Jonathan Fielden: I think we have proved over

00:19:18.770 --> 00:19:26.360
the last few years how we have improved year
on year, landing the budget in 2015; that

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:30.360
we have done that through strengthening the
teams, but also through strengthening the

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:37.400
conversations that we are having between the
teams, particularly our hub teams, and providers,

00:19:37.400 --> 00:19:43.440
around how best to utilise the funds that
we have. We will need to continue that, going

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:49.360
forward. How we have changed, as the Board
agreed a couple of months back, the Cancer

00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:54.510
Drugs Fund to make sure that we have the best
drugs coming in, but also recognising when

00:19:54.510 --> 00:20:00.840
they need to come out in a sustainable way.
How we work with NICE to ensure that we have

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:06.780
the right drugs coming through in an affordable
way, so that means that we can deliver for

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:12.520
patients that need it in a balanced way, rather
than allowing all the funds to come through

00:20:12.530 --> 00:20:17.630
into specialised.
It will be challenging. I think Paul has said

00:20:17.630 --> 00:20:22.130
at other times at the Board that this is one
of the most challenging areas of our budget.

00:20:22.130 --> 00:20:27.360
However, I believe we have the teams in place,
we have real conversations, as can be seen

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:33.550
from the contracting rounds this year, and
we are determined to go ahead with the outlined

00:20:33.550 --> 00:20:37.300
strategy to maintain that going forward. It
will remain challenging.

00:20:37.300 --> 00:20:41.270
Paul Baumann: I have little to add, really,
because I think you have made most of the

00:20:41.270 --> 00:20:44.510
points I want to make. The only thing that
is perhaps worth making a link to is the Right

00:20:44.510 --> 00:20:51.570
Care Programme, which we often think of as
being a CCG-oriented or local health economy‑oriented

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:56.300
programme, which it undoubtedly is. However,
it absolutely has applications for specialised

00:20:56.300 --> 00:21:01.540
commissioning to ensure that, in this national
set of services as a whole, we have the consistency

00:21:01.540 --> 00:21:06.980
in what we do. I think that will be an important
part of it, not over the next month or two

00:21:06.980 --> 00:21:15.460
but over the next few months, we will get
that underway and it will have its impact.

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:17.180
David Roberts: I think, Malcolm, the Audit
and Risk Committee will at some stage want

00:21:17.180 --> 00:21:22.400
to have a deep dive onto how we are managing
the risks associated with specialised, because

00:21:22.400 --> 00:21:26.990
in my internal risk register this is right
up there at the top.

00:21:26.990 --> 00:21:34.290
Malcolm Grant: Are there any other comments
or questions? No? In that case, Jonathan thank

00:21:34.290 --> 00:21:40.510
you very much for the presentation. The Board
has expressed I think a number of interests

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:46.470
and anxieties, and risks, accommodating what
David has just said, and we will want to keep

00:21:46.470 --> 00:21:52.230
it closely under observation, but you have
given us your assurances today. Can I invite

00:21:52.230 --> 00:21:58.780
the Board then to agree the next steps, which
are set out effectively in the paper, on the

00:21:58.780 --> 00:22:03.660
basis of the presentation we have heard today?
Good? Thank you all very much. Thank you very

00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:04.600
much, Jonathan.

